"""Apply original revision content to the existing canonical Biology catalog.

Reference boundaries independently checked in IB's Biology guide (2025 first
assessment), printed pp. 67-71. This does not reproduce the guide's prose,
figures or questions. Numerical datasets below are synthetic practice data.
Existing resource keys are retained so saved review history is not replaced.
"""
import json
from pathlib import Path

path = Path('IBVault/Materials/Biology/C.json')
topics = json.loads(path.read_text())
by_code = {topic['code']: topic for topic in topics}


def section(code, key, title, points, body, pitfall, hl=False):
    return dict(key=key, title=title, syllabusPoints=[f'{code}.{i}' for i in points],
                body=body, pitfall=pitfall, hlOnly=hl)


def question(key, section_key, kind, command, prompt, answer, explanation,
             distractors=(), hl=False):
    return dict(key=key, sectionKey=section_key, kind=kind, command=command,
                prompt=prompt, answer=answer, distractors=list(distractors),
                explanation=explanation, hlOnly=hl)


r = by_code['C1.2']
old_r = {q['key']: q for q in r['questions']}
r['sections'] = [
    section('C1.2', 'atp', 'ATP as a shared energy carrier', [1, 2],
        'ATP (adenosine triphosphate) is a nucleotide containing adenine, ribose and three phosphate groups. Cells use ATP as an immediately available energy carrier, not as their main long-term energy reserve. Both autotrophs and heterotrophs use it.\n\nATP-dependent reactions support active transport against concentration gradients, synthesis of larger molecules from smaller ones, and movement such as muscle contraction or movement along a cytoskeleton. Enzymes couple ATP use to these processes rather than releasing energy at an unrelated location. Plants continue to require ATP for cellular work in darkness as well as in light.',
        'ATP is neither an enzyme nor a store of energy that is used only by animals.'),
    section('C1.2', 'atp-cycle', 'ATP use and regeneration', [3],
        'ATP + water → ADP + inorganic phosphate is an energy-releasing hydrolysis overall. The reverse conversion, ADP + phosphate → ATP, requires an energy input. Respiration supplies energy for much of this regeneration; photosynthesis also produces ATP within chloroplasts.\n\nATP and ADP are continually recycled. A small ATP pool can support extensive cellular work because its molecules are regenerated repeatedly. An ATP concentration that remains steady therefore does not imply that ATP is unused. If ATP production stops while ATP-dependent work continues, the ATP pool falls.\n\nBond breaking itself requires energy. ATP hydrolysis releases energy overall because the complete reaction, including formation and stabilization of products, is energetically favourable.',
        'Do not explain ATP hydrolysis as energy being released simply by breaking a bond.'),
    section('C1.2', 'pathways', 'Respiration in human cells', [4, 5],
        'Cell respiration is a sequence of enzyme-controlled reactions transferring energy from carbon compounds into ATP and heat. It is not the same process as ventilation or exchange of oxygen and carbon dioxide. Glucose and, in aerobic pathways, fatty acids can be respiratory substrates.\n\nA simplified aerobic equation is C6H12O6 + 6 O2 → 6 CO2 + 6 H2O. In human cells, glucose breakdown starts in the cytoplasm and aerobic stages continue in mitochondria. Aerobic respiration has a much larger ATP yield per glucose than the anaerobic route.\n\nHuman cells can obtain a small amount of ATP by converting glucose to lactate without using oxygen. This occurs in the cytoplasm, helps working muscles when demand exceeds oxygen supply, and supplies mature red blood cells, which have no mitochondria. Lactate formation does not release carbon dioxide.',
        'Aerobic and anaerobic pathways are not simply switched on and off throughout the whole body together.'),
    section('C1.2', 'respirometry', 'Measuring a respiration rate', [6],
        'A respirometer can estimate oxygen uptake from a change in gas volume. An absorbent removes carbon dioxide so that its release does not offset oxygen uptake. Keep the absorbent separate from living tissue. Check seals and allow temperature and pressure to equilibrate before measuring.\n\nRate = change in oxygen volume / elapsed time. Divide by tissue mass when comparing unequal samples. For example, uptake of 0.48 cm³ in 4 minutes by 2.0 g of seeds is 0.060 cm³ g⁻¹ min⁻¹. With a capillary, volume change equals movement distance multiplied by its cross-sectional area.\n\nAn identical apparatus containing inert beads or killed tissue helps detect physical changes unrelated to living respiration. Match occupied volume, hold temperature and other conditions constant, use independent replicates, and report variation. Oxygen uptake alone does not measure anaerobic ATP production.',
        'Gas contraction is not automatically oxygen uptake if carbon dioxide, leaks or temperature changes are uncontrolled.'),
    section('C1.2', 'redox', 'Hydrogen and electron carriers', [7],
        'Oxidation is loss of electrons; reduction is gain of electrons. During respiration, removal of hydrogen from a carbon compound is an oxidation, and a carrier accepts the electrons. NAD+ is the oxidized form of a carrier. NAD+ + 2 electrons + H+ → NADH represents its reduction; another hydrogen ion may be released into the surrounding solution during substrate oxidation.\n\nNADH carries reducing power to another reaction and is oxidized back to NAD+. These are forms of the same recyclable carrier, not different energy sources created from nothing. Continued oxidation of substrate requires a supply of the oxidized carrier. NADH is not ATP and does not directly substitute for ATP in most ATP-dependent work.',
        'Reduced NAD means NAD has gained electrons, not that its quantity has decreased.', True),
    section('C1.2', 'glycolysis', 'Following glucose through glycolysis', [8],
        'Glycolysis occurs in the cytoplasm. A six-carbon glucose molecule is phosphorylated using two ATP, then split into two three-carbon intermediates. Their oxidation reduces NAD+ to NADH. Subsequent reactions transfer phosphate directly from intermediates to ADP, producing four ATP by substrate-level phosphorylation.\n\nPer glucose, the net result is two pyruvate, two NADH and two ATP: four ATP are made but two were invested. The six carbon atoms remain in the two three-carbon pyruvate molecules, so glycolysis releases no carbon dioxide. It does not use oxygen directly, but it can continue only if NAD+ is regenerated from NADH.',
        'Four ATP formed is the gross output, not the net ATP yield of glycolysis.', True),
    section('C1.2', 'fermentation', 'Keeping glycolysis supplied with NAD+', [9, 10],
        'When pyruvate is reduced to lactate in human cells, NADH is oxidized to NAD+. This regenerates the carrier required by glycolysis. The conversion to lactate adds no extra ATP; the net yield remains two ATP per glucose from glycolysis.\n\nIn yeast, pyruvate loses carbon dioxide and the remaining two-carbon compound is reduced to ethanol, regenerating NAD+. Ethanol and carbon dioxide are useful products in brewing and bread making: carbon dioxide expands dough, while ethanol is the desired product in alcoholic fermentation.\n\nThe human lactate pathway and yeast ethanol pathway therefore differ in carbon products, although both maintain glycolytic ATP production without oxygen. If the NAD+ pool is exhausted, glycolysis cannot sustain ATP production merely because glucose remains.',
        'Human lactate formation does not generate the carbon dioxide produced in the yeast pathway.', True),
    section('C1.2', 'link', 'From pyruvate to acetyl-CoA', [11],
        'In eukaryotic cells, pyruvate enters the mitochondrial matrix. Each three-carbon pyruvate loses one carbon as carbon dioxide, is oxidized while NAD+ becomes NADH, and supplies a two-carbon acetyl group that joins coenzyme A. This oxidative decarboxylation is the link reaction.\n\nFor the two pyruvate produced from one glucose, the link reaction produces two acetyl-CoA, two carbon dioxide and two NADH. It does not directly produce ATP. Coenzyme A temporarily carries an acetyl group; its whole molecule is not consumed as a two-carbon respiratory substrate. Acetyl-CoA feeds the acetyl group into the Krebs cycle.',
        'Do not count link-reaction carbon dioxide as a product of glycolysis.', True),
    section('C1.2', 'krebs', 'Carbon and carriers in the Krebs cycle', [12],
        'In the mitochondrial matrix, a two-carbon acetyl group combines with four-carbon oxaloacetate to form six-carbon citrate. Reactions involving oxidation and two decarboxylations regenerate the four-carbon acceptor, enabling another turn.\n\nOne turn per acetyl group releases two carbon dioxide and forms three NADH, one reduced FAD (FADH2), and one ATP equivalent by substrate-level phosphorylation. Reduced FAD accounts for a fourth oxidation, alongside the three that reduce NAD+. One glucose supplies two acetyl groups, so these quantities double per glucose. Most of the energy transferred at this stage is in reduced carriers rather than directly formed ATP.\n\nThe cycle regenerates oxaloacetate; it does not double the starting acceptor on each turn. Oxygen is not directly consumed in the cycle, but continued operation depends on reoxidation of its reduced carriers.',
        'Two carbon atoms enter and two leave per turn, but they need not be the very same atoms in the first turn.', True),
    section('C1.2', 'electron-transport', 'Electron flow and the proton gradient', [13, 14, 16],
        'NADH donates electrons to carriers in the inner mitochondrial membrane and is oxidized to NAD+. Reduced FAD also supplies electrons. Successive electron transfers release energy that drives proton movement from the matrix into the intermembrane space. The membrane restricts free proton movement, allowing an electrochemical gradient to form.\n\nOxygen is the final electron acceptor. It combines with electrons and protons from the matrix to form water. Removing oxygen blocks sustained electron flow, inhibits regeneration of oxidized carriers and eventually slows the link reaction and Krebs cycle.\n\nElectron flow and proton flow are related but not identical: electrons pass between carriers, while protons are pumped across the membrane. Cristae provide a large area for these membrane proteins.',
        'Oxygen does not directly split glucose or carry protons through ATP synthase.', True),
    section('C1.2', 'chemiosmosis', 'Using a gradient to make ATP', [15],
        'Protons move down their electrochemical gradient from the intermembrane space to the matrix through ATP synthase. Their passage drives changes in the enzyme that couple ADP and inorganic phosphate to form ATP. This coupling of a proton gradient to ATP formation is chemiosmosis, part of oxidative phosphorylation.\n\nA proton leak can dissipate the gradient even while electrons continue along the transport chain. ATP production then falls because less proton flow is coupled through ATP synthase. By contrast, blocking ATP synthase initially steepens the gradient; as pumping against that gradient becomes difficult, electron transport and oxygen consumption can slow.\n\nAn intact inner membrane is therefore essential for efficient ATP synthesis. Electron transfer alone is insufficient if the energy cannot be retained in a usable gradient.',
        'A larger oxygen-consumption rate does not always imply a larger ATP-production rate, especially during uncoupling.', True),
    section('C1.2', 'fuels', 'Comparing respiratory substrates', [17],
        'Both carbohydrates and lipids can supply carbon for aerobic respiration. Fatty acids are broken into two-carbon acetyl groups carried by coenzyme A; they can then enter the Krebs cycle. Fatty-acid breakdown also supplies reduced carriers.\n\nLipids contain more carbon-hydrogen bonds and less oxygen relative to their mass than carbohydrates, so their complete oxidation generally releases more energy per gram. This makes fat an effective energy reserve. A per-gram comparison is not a per-molecule comparison: molecular masses differ.\n\nHuman anaerobic glycolytic ATP production depends on carbohydrate. Fatty acids cannot replace glucose to maintain that pathway when oxygen supply limits aerobic ATP production. Aerobic ATP yield also depends on coupling and transport, so a single rigid ATP total should not be assumed for all cells.',
        'A fuel with a higher energy yield per gram is not necessarily usable by every cell under every condition.', True),
]
