r['remaining'] = ('All 17 numbered understandings now have relevant teaching and linked practice, with core 1–6 and HL 7–17 separated. This is still partial revision support: add labelled mitochondrial/pathway diagrams, a wider range of unfamiliar respirometer traces and uncertainty analyses, practical execution and independent teacher review. References do not certify every assessment demand.')

p = by_code['C1.3']
old_p = {q['key']: q for q in p['questions']}
p['sections'] = [
    section('C1.3', 'transformation', 'Energy input and carbon products', [1, 2, 3],
        'Photosynthesis transfers light energy into chemical energy in organic compounds. Carbon dioxide supplies carbon; water supplies hydrogen in oxygenic photosynthesis. A simplified overall equation is 6 CO2 + 6 H2O → C6H12O6 + 6 O2, with light and photosynthetic pigments required. The detailed pathway builds smaller carbon compounds before sugars are assembled.\n\nPlants, algae and cyanobacteria perform oxygenic photosynthesis. Oxygen released as a by-product originates from water, not directly from carbon dioxide. Light supplies energy, not the carbon atoms in biomass.\n\nPlants also respire in light and darkness. Measured oxygen release can therefore represent net photosynthesis: gross photosynthetic oxygen production minus respiratory oxygen use.',
        'The simplified equation is not a claim that glucose is formed in one reaction.'),
    section('C1.3', 'chromatography', 'Separating photosynthetic pigments', [4],
        'In chromatography, dissolved pigments move with a solvent over a stationary material. Different pigments have different relative affinities for these phases and therefore travel different distances. Apply a small concentrated spot above the starting solvent level and mark the solvent front before it evaporates.\n\nRf = distance travelled by pigment from the origin / distance travelled by solvent front from the same origin. Measure to the centre of a pigment band. If a band travels 3.6 cm and the front 6.0 cm, Rf = 0.60, with no units.\n\nColour and Rf can support identification using standards run under the same conditions. Rf depends on the solvent and stationary phase, so a remembered value alone cannot establish identity across different methods.',
        'Measure from the origin, not the bottom of the paper, and do not treat Rf as a distance.'),
    section('C1.3', 'pigments', 'Absorption spectra and action spectra', [5, 6],
        'A pigment absorbs particular wavelengths of visible light; absorbed photons can raise electrons to higher energy states. Chlorophyll absorbs strongly in blue and red regions and less strongly in green, much of which is reflected or transmitted. Accessory pigments extend the wavelengths available.\n\nAn absorption spectrum plots how strongly a pigment or sample absorbs light against wavelength, usually in nanometres. An action spectrum plots photosynthetic activity, such as oxygen production, against wavelength. The vertical axes measure different things even when the overall patterns resemble each other.\n\nTo compare effects of wavelength, control the photon flux, temperature and carbon-dioxide availability. A whole-leaf action spectrum reflects multiple pigments and other biological factors, not only purified chlorophyll.',
        'Low absorption of green light does not mean every leaf has exactly zero photosynthesis in green light.'),
    section('C1.3', 'limiting', 'Interpreting limiting factors', [7],
        'Light intensity, carbon-dioxide availability and temperature can limit photosynthetic rate. Increasing one factor increases the rate only while that factor constrains the process. A plateau after increasing light may indicate that carbon dioxide or another condition is now limiting; the plateau alone does not identify which one.\n\nChange one factor at a time, control the others, allow equilibration and use independent replicates. A lamp can alter temperature as well as light intensity, so temperature must be measured and controlled.\n\nGas-exchange measurements need interpretation: oxygen-bubble counts are less precise than gas volume or concentration because bubble sizes vary. Net oxygen release combines photosynthetic production and respiratory use. An effect on net exchange does not automatically establish an identical effect on gross photosynthesis.',
        'A rising factor is not necessarily still limiting after the response has reached a plateau.'),
    section('C1.3', 'enrichment', 'Testing effects of elevated carbon dioxide', [8],
        'Growth chambers and greenhouses allow controlled changes in carbon dioxide while light, water and temperature are regulated. They help isolate causal effects, but enclosure conditions can differ from natural environments.\n\nFree-air carbon-dioxide enrichment (FACE) exposes plants to elevated carbon dioxide outdoors without enclosing them. It preserves more natural interactions with weather, soil and surrounding organisms, but controlling exposure and other conditions is harder. Replicated treatment and ambient-control plots improve interpretation.\n\nHigher carbon dioxide can increase photosynthesis when carbon availability limits it, but responses also depend on nutrients, water, temperature and acclimation. Results from one species and setting do not establish that all ecosystems will respond identically. Distinguish a short-term leaf rate from long-term biomass production.',
        'More realistic field conditions do not remove the need for controls and replication.'),
    section('C1.3', 'photosystems', 'Capturing light in photosystems', [9, 10],
        'Photosystems are pigment-protein complexes in thylakoid membranes. Antenna pigments absorb light and transfer excitation energy towards a reaction centre. A reaction-centre chlorophyll can then donate an excited electron to an acceptor, beginning directed electron transfer.\n\nMany antenna pigments can deliver energy to one reaction centre, increasing the probability of capturing usable light. Different accessory pigments absorb different wavelength ranges, broadening capture beyond that of a single chlorophyll molecule.\n\nEnergy transfer among antenna pigments is not the same as the electron-transfer sequence leaving the reaction centre. Photosystem II operates before photosystem I in the usual non-cyclic pathway, despite their numerical names.',
        'Antenna pigments collect excitation energy; they do not each generate a separate ATP molecule.', True),
    section('C1.3', 'photolysis', 'Replacing photosystem II electrons', [11],
        'Light excites electrons in photosystem II and electron transfer removes them from its reaction centre. Water is split at the photosystem-II-associated complex to replace those electrons. The products are electrons, protons and oxygen.\n\nThe balanced relationship is 2 H2O → O2 + 4 H+ + 4 electrons. Protons released on the thylakoid-lumen side contribute to the proton gradient, and oxygen can leave as a by-product. Sustained non-cyclic flow needs this electron supply.\n\nWater supplies the electrons; carbon dioxide has a different role in carbon fixation. Isotopic labelling of water can therefore trace the origin of the oxygen gas.',
        'Photolysis is associated with photosystem II, not the Calvin cycle or photosystem I.', True),
    section('C1.3', 'light-atp', 'Photophosphorylation and proton flow', [12],
        'Electrons leaving photosystem II pass through carriers towards photosystem I. Electron-transfer energy supports proton movement from the stroma into the thylakoid lumen. Water splitting also supplies lumen protons. The membrane limits proton leakage.\n\nProtons return down their electrochemical gradient through ATP synthase from the lumen to the stroma, driving ATP formation on the stromal side. This light-supported ATP formation is photophosphorylation.\n\nIn non-cyclic flow, water supplies electrons and the pathway ultimately produces reduced NADP as well as ATP, with oxygen released. In cyclic flow, electrons excited at photosystem I return through carriers. Cyclic flow can support additional ATP formation without net reduced NADP production or water splitting by that cycle.',
        'The lumen-to-stroma flow through ATP synthase is opposite to the energy-driven pumping direction.', True),
    section('C1.3', 'nadph', 'Photosystem I and reduced NADP', [13],
        'Light raises electrons in photosystem I to a higher energy state. In non-cyclic flow, electrons arrive to replace those lost by its reaction centre, while the excited electrons pass to acceptors that enable NADP+ reduction. NADP+ accepts two electrons and a proton from the stroma to form NADPH, also called reduced NADP.\n\nNADPH transfers reducing power to reactions in the stroma, including reduction of carbon compounds during the Calvin cycle. It is then oxidized to NADP+, which can accept electrons again. ATP and NADPH have different roles: ATP supplies energy through coupled reactions, whereas NADPH supplies reducing power.',
        'NADPH is not an alternative name for ATP, and reduced NADP is not the carrier used in glycolysis.', True),
    section('C1.3', 'spatial', 'Chloroplast compartments', [14],
        'The thylakoid membrane holds photosystems, electron carriers and ATP synthase in an organized arrangement. The thylakoid lumen is a small compartment in which proton concentration can increase. The stroma lies outside the thylakoids but inside the chloroplast envelope.\n\nLight-dependent reactions build the proton gradient across the thylakoid membrane, while ATP and NADPH become available on the stromal side. Enzymes for the Calvin cycle act in the stroma, where these products can be used. Thylakoid membranes provide a large working area and separate the two sides of the gradient.\n\nRupturing the membrane can eliminate efficient chemiosmotic ATP production even if some isolated pigments still absorb light.',
        'The stroma is not the fluid inside the thylakoid lumen.', True),
    section('C1.3', 'fixation', 'Rubisco and carbon entry', [15],
        'Carbon fixation incorporates inorganic carbon dioxide into an organic compound. In the stroma, the enzyme Rubisco catalyses addition of one carbon dioxide to five-carbon ribulose bisphosphate (RuBP). The unstable six-carbon product splits into two molecules of three-carbon glycerate 3-phosphate (GP).\n\nFor three carbon dioxide molecules, three RuBP molecules are used and six GP molecules result. The carbon count is 3 + 15 = 18, matching six three-carbon products. RuBP is the carbon-dioxide acceptor; Rubisco is the enzyme. Their similar names do not indicate identical roles.\n\nFixation alone is not completion of sugar synthesis: the products still need reduction and the acceptor must be regenerated.',
        'Carbon dioxide does not combine directly with ATP to become glucose.', True),
    section('C1.3', 'reduction', 'Producing triose phosphate', [16],
        'Glycerate 3-phosphate is converted to triose phosphate (TP) using ATP and reduced NADP. ATP supports the energetically demanding conversion, and NADPH supplies reducing power. ADP and NADP+ return to the pool available to the light-dependent reactions.\n\nFor fixation of three carbon dioxide molecules, conversion of the six GP molecules uses six ATP and six NADPH to form six TP molecules. Each TP contains three carbons. Most TP stays in the cycle to regenerate the carbon acceptor; only a fraction represents net carbon gain available for other synthesis.\n\nThe term light-independent does not mean these reactions usually run indefinitely in darkness: their supplies of ATP and NADPH depend on the light-dependent reactions.',
        'GP and TP both contain three carbons, but they differ in chemical state and cannot be treated as the same compound.', True),
    section('C1.3', 'regeneration', 'Recycling RuBP and counting net gain', [17],
        'For every three carbon dioxide fixed, six three-carbon TP molecules form. Five TP, containing fifteen carbons in total, regenerate three five-carbon RuBP molecules. Regeneration uses a further three ATP. The remaining one TP, containing three carbons, is the net product available to leave this cycle accounting.\n\nCombining reduction and regeneration gives nine ATP and six NADPH used per net TP from three carbon dioxide. Producing the carbon equivalent of a six-carbon sugar requires two net TP, so the totals double.\n\nFive-sixths of the TP produced is therefore recycled in this accounting. Removing all six TP as products would exhaust RuBP and prevent continued fixation.',
        'Count the net exported TP, not every TP molecule temporarily formed during the cycle.', True),
    section('C1.3', 'products', 'From fixed carbon to biomass', [18],
        'Products of the Calvin cycle provide carbon skeletons for the synthesis of other organic compounds. Triose phosphate can contribute to hexose formation; those sugars can be used to make sucrose for transport, starch for storage and cellulose for cell walls. Carbon skeletons also contribute to lipid and amino-acid synthesis.\n\nPhotosynthesis alone does not supply every element in biomass. Amino acids and proteins require nitrogen, obtained for example through assimilation of absorbed nitrate or ammonium. Other compounds require mineral nutrients such as phosphorus or sulfur.\n\nA plant can therefore be limited in growth by mineral supply even when light and carbon dioxide permit carbon fixation. Net biomass increase also depends on respiration and allocation of products.',
        'Carbon dioxide and water cannot by themselves supply the nitrogen atoms in proteins.', True),
    section('C1.3', 'interdependence', 'Why the reaction groups depend on each other', [19],
        'Light-dependent reactions supply ATP and NADPH to the Calvin cycle. In using them, the cycle regenerates ADP, phosphate and NADP+, which can support continued light-dependent reactions. The pathways exchange carriers as well as carbon and energy.\n\nWhen light is removed, new production of ATP and NADPH falls, so sustained Calvin-cycle activity becomes limited as available supplies are used. When carbon-dioxide supply is severely restricted, carbon fixation and consumption of ATP and NADPH can fall. Slower regeneration of NADP+ and ADP can then limit sustained electron flow and ATP formation.\n\nThese are linked responses, not claims that every intermediate becomes zero instantly. Storage pools and alternative electron pathways can affect the short-term response.',
        'Light-independent reactions are not independent of the products of light-dependent reactions.', True),
]
